 <iati-activity default-currency="USD" hierarchy="1" humanitarian="0" iati-activities:version="2.03" xml:lang="EN" xmlns:iati-activities="http://d-portal.org/xmlns/iati-activities">
  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-47136-TG02</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-47136" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Global Green Growth Institute</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Resilience to Climate Change of Vulnerable Population through Sustainable Forestry Resources Management</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Project aims at improving socio-economic resilience and reducing poverty among women and youth involved in Shea products business through improved organizational structure and governance, increased plantation population of Shea trees in available land within or near the existing parkland and strengthened Shea products value chains to creating enabling environment for investment (production, processing, and market access).The Project aims to directly benefit 15,000 women and youth engaged in shea production, collection, and processing, including the creation of 5000 green jobs. The shea tree is a multipurpose tree crop indigenous to Sub Saharan Africa. The gross income per woman collector is at USD 75 annually. Through shea parklands expansion and improvement of collector productivity, the gross income per woman collector could increase to USD 167 per year. Shea trees grow naturally and are integrated with crops on smallholder farms, creating an agroforestry landscape that acts like a carbon sink.Outcome 1 &amp;ndash; Local Governance and Knowledge Management of the Shea parklands strengthened Outcome 2 &amp;ndash; Reforestation of high-quality shea trees and providing technical guidanceOutcome 3 &amp;ndash; Establishment of a screening and post-harvest management system to improve incomeOutcome 4 &amp;ndash; Improved protection and delivery of shea landscape scale mitigation, adaptation and climate resilience for climate change through direct engagement of women and youth cooperatives, integration with inclusive livelihood approaches and early warning services</narrative>
  </description>
